// Computer Networks, Unit IV: the transport layer.
// Follows CN - Notes3 (Unit IV) and Tanenbaum chapter 6.
export default {
  'cn.u4.transport-service': {
    oneLine: 'The transport layer gives application processes efficient, reliable, cost-effective, end-to-end (process-to-process) delivery on top of an unreliable network, through a small set of service primitives.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Transport layer', text: 'The layer between the application and network layers that provides **process-to-process** communication between two application processes, one on the local host and one on the remote host, over a **logical connection**. It is the heart of the TCP/IP suite.' },
      { def: 'Transport entity', text: 'The hardware and/or software within the transport layer that does the work. It can be in the operating system kernel, in a library bound into applications, in a separate user process, or on the network interface card.' },
      { h: 'Services provided to the upper layers' },
      'The ultimate goal is to provide **efficient, reliable and cost-effective** data transmission to its users (processes in the application layer), using the services of the network layer. Like the network layer, it offers two types of service:',
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Connection-oriented transport service', items: ['Three phases: establishment, data transfer, release', 'Reliable: lost and damaged data is recovered', 'Addressing and flow control similar to the network layer', 'Example: TCP'] },
        { title: 'Connectionless transport service', items: ['Each message sent independently', 'No setup; no guarantee of delivery or order', 'Hard to build on top of a connection-oriented network (setting up a connection for one packet is wasteful)', 'Example: UDP'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Why a transport layer if the network layer already exists?' },
      { ul: ['The transport code runs entirely on the **users\' machines**, while the network layer mostly runs on routers operated by the carrier. Users have no control over the network layer, so they cannot fix poor service there.', 'The transport layer can **improve the quality of service**: if packets are lost or a router crashes, the transport entity can set up a new connection, ask which data arrived, and continue.', 'It **isolates** applications from the technology, design and imperfections of the network: programs can be written against a standard set of primitives that work on any network.'] },
      'Layers 1 to 4 are therefore called the **transport service provider**, and the layers above the **transport service user**.',
      { h: 'Network service vs transport service' },
      { table: { head: ['Point', 'Network service', 'Transport service'], rows: [['Models', 'What real networks offer', 'What applications need'], ['Reliability', 'Generally unreliable: real networks lose packets', 'Connection-oriented service is **reliable**: that is its purpose'], ['Used by', 'Only transport entities', 'Many programs and programmers']] } },
      { h: 'Transport service primitives' },
      'To let applications use the service, the transport layer provides operations (a transport service interface). A simple, hypothetical set:',
      { table: { head: ['Primitive', 'Segment sent', 'Meaning'], rows: [['**LISTEN**', '(none)', 'Block until some process tries to connect'], ['**CONNECT**', 'CONNECTION REQ.', 'Actively attempt to establish a connection'], ['**SEND**', 'DATA', 'Send information'], ['**RECEIVE**', '(none)', 'Block until a DATA segment arrives'], ['**DISCONNECT**', 'DISCONNECTION REQ.', 'This side wants to release the connection']] } },
      'Messages sent from transport entity to transport entity are called **segments** (TPDUs, transport protocol data units). Segments are carried inside network-layer packets, which are carried inside data link frames: the nesting is **frame ⊃ packet ⊃ segment**.',
      { img: 'cn-nesting' },
      { example: 'A server executes LISTEN and blocks. A client executes CONNECT, sending a CONNECTION REQUEST segment; the server unblocks and sends back CONNECTION ACCEPTED, and the connection is established. Data is exchanged with SEND and RECEIVE. When done, either side executes DISCONNECT.' },
      { h: 'Typical transport-layer duties' },
      { ul: ['**Process-to-process delivery** using port numbers.', '**Multiplexing and demultiplexing** many connections over one network address.', '**Segmentation and reassembly** of messages.', '**Connection control**, **flow control** and **error control** end to end.', '**Congestion control** (TCP).'] },
    ],
    terms: [['Transport entity', 'The software or hardware that implements the transport layer.'], ['Segment (TPDU)', 'The unit of data exchanged between transport entities.'], ['Process-to-process', 'Delivery to a specific program on a host, identified by a port.']],
    exam: 'Define the transport layer and transport entity, explain the two kinds of service, explain why the transport layer is needed although the network layer exists, list the primitives in a table with their segments, and show the frame–packet–segment nesting.',
    revise: ['Process-to-process, end-to-end', 'Connection-oriented (TCP) vs connectionless (UDP)', 'LISTEN, CONNECT, SEND, RECEIVE, DISCONNECT', 'Frame ⊃ packet ⊃ segment'],
  },

  'cn.u4.sockets': {
    oneLine: 'Berkeley sockets are the standard programming interface to TCP and UDP: a server calls socket, bind, listen and accept; a client calls socket and connect; both then send and receive and finally close.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Socket', text: 'An endpoint of communication: the combination of an **IP address and a port number**. The **Berkeley socket** primitives, first released with Berkeley UNIX 4.2BSD in 1983, are the standard API for Internet programming (Windows uses a socket-style API called **Winsock**).' },
      { h: 'The socket primitives for TCP' },
      { table: { head: ['Primitive', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**SOCKET**', 'Create a new communication endpoint (returns a file descriptor). Parameters give the addressing format, the service type (e.g. reliable byte stream) and the protocol.'],
        ['**BIND**', 'Associate a local address (IP and port) with a socket, so remote clients can connect to it.'],
        ['**LISTEN**', 'Announce willingness to accept connections; give the queue size for incoming requests. It does not block.'],
        ['**ACCEPT**', 'Passively establish an incoming connection: block until a connection request arrives, then create a **new socket** for it and return its descriptor, so the server can go back to accept more.'],
        ['**CONNECT**', 'Actively attempt to establish a connection to a remote socket; blocks until the connection is set up (the three-way handshake).'],
        ['**SEND**', 'Send some data over the connection.'],
        ['**RECEIVE**', 'Receive some data from the connection.'],
        ['**CLOSE**', 'Release the connection. TCP release is symmetric: the connection is released when both sides have closed.'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Order of calls' },
      { img: 'cn-sockets' },
      { steps: [
        'Server: **socket()** creates the endpoint, **bind()** gives it a well-known port (e.g. 80), **listen()** allocates a queue, then **accept()** blocks.',
        'Client: **socket()**, then **connect()** to the server\'s IP and port. This triggers the TCP three-way handshake.',
        'When the connection is set up, the server\'s accept() returns a new socket for this client; the client\'s connect() returns.',
        'Both use **send()** and **receive()** (in C: write/read, send/recv) to exchange data; the connection is full-duplex.',
        'Both call **close()**. A concurrent server usually hands each accepted connection to a new process or thread and loops back to accept().',
      ] },
      { h: 'UDP sockets' },
      'For UDP (connectionless), there is no listen, accept or connect: the server calls **socket()** and **bind()**, then **recvfrom()**; the client calls **socket()** and **sendto()** with the destination address on every datagram.',
      { h: 'TCP sockets vs UDP sockets (asked in Nov 2025)' },
      { table: { head: ['Point', 'TCP socket (SOCK_STREAM)', 'UDP socket (SOCK_DGRAM)'], rows: [['Connection', 'Connection-oriented: connect/accept first', 'Connectionless: no setup'], ['Calls', 'socket, bind, listen, accept, connect, send, recv, close', 'socket, bind, sendto, recvfrom, close'], ['Data', 'Byte stream; message boundaries not preserved', 'Datagrams; boundaries preserved'], ['Reliability', 'Reliable, ordered, no duplicates', 'May be lost, duplicated or reordered'], ['Sockets per client on the server', 'A new socket per accepted connection', 'One socket serves all clients'], ['Uses', 'Web, e-mail, file transfer', 'DNS, streaming, online games']] } },
      { example: 'An Internet file server (Tanenbaum\'s example): the server creates a socket, binds it to port 12345, listens, and loops: accept a connection, read the file name the client sends, open the file, write its contents to the socket in blocks, and close the connection. The client connects, sends the file name, and reads blocks until end of file.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Socket', 'IP address + port: an endpoint for communication.'], ['File descriptor', 'The integer handle a socket call returns, used like an open file.'], ['Concurrent server', 'A server that handles each client in a separate process or thread.']],
    exam: 'Define a socket, give the table of TCP socket primitives with their meanings, draw the server and client call sequence with the connection and data arrows, and compare TCP and UDP sockets.',
    revise: ['Server: socket → bind → listen → accept → recv/send → close', 'Client: socket → connect → send/recv → close', 'accept returns a new socket per client', 'UDP: sendto/recvfrom, no connect'],
  },

  'cn.u4.elements': {
    oneLine: 'Transport protocols, like data link protocols, handle addressing, connection establishment, connection release, error and flow control, multiplexing and crash recovery, but over a whole network that can delay, duplicate and reorder packets.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Transport protocol', text: 'The protocol used between two transport entities to implement the transport service. It resembles a data link protocol (error control, sequencing, flow control), but works over a **whole subnet** rather than one physical line.' },
      { h: 'Why transport protocols are harder than data link protocols' },
      { table: { head: ['Data link layer', 'Transport layer'], rows: [['Two routers communicate directly over a physical channel', 'The physical channel is replaced by the entire network'], ['No need to say which router you want: each line leads to one router', '**Explicit addressing** of destinations is required'], ['Connection setup is simple: the other end is always there', 'Initial **connection establishment is complicated**'], ['A frame either arrives or is lost', 'The network can **store packets** and deliver them much later (delayed duplicates)'], ['Fixed number of buffers per line', 'Many connections with varying bandwidth: **buffering and flow control** are more complex']] } },
      { h: '1. Addressing' },
      'A process must say which remote process it wants to talk to. Transport addresses that processes listen on are called **ports** in the Internet; the generic term is **TSAP** (Transport Service Access Point). Network-layer addresses (IP addresses) are **NSAPs** (Network Service Access Points).',
      { steps: ['A mail server process attaches itself to **TSAP 1522** on host 2 and waits (LISTEN).', 'An application on host 1 attaches to **TSAP 1208** and issues CONNECT, naming TSAP 1208 on host 1 as the source and TSAP 1522 on host 2 as the destination.', 'A transport connection is set up; the application sends the mail message.', 'The server replies that it will deliver it.', 'The transport connection is released.'] },
      'How does the client know the server\'s TSAP? Well-known services use fixed ports (in /etc/services); others use a **portmapper** (directory server) or a **process server** (inetd) that listens on many ports and starts the right server on demand.',
      { h: '2. Connection establishment' },
      'Simply sending CONNECTION REQUEST and waiting for CONNECTION ACCEPTED fails when the network can lose, delay, corrupt and **duplicate** packets. The nightmare: a user tells a bank to transfer money; the packets take the scenic route, the sender times out and resends, the copies arrive quickly and the connection is released; then the old packets finally arrive and the bank transfers the money **again**. The crux: **delayed duplicates** are thought to be new packets.',
      { ul: ['**Throwaway transport addresses** (a new one per connection): duplicates find no process, but it is then hard to connect in the first place.', '**Unique connection identifiers** remembered in a table of obsolete connections: requires each entity to keep history forever, lost in a crash.', '**Limit packet lifetime** to a known maximum T, by restricted network design, a hop counter, or timestamps. Then sequence numbers that are not reused within T (using a clock-based initial sequence number) can reject old duplicates.'] },
      'TCP combines limited lifetime with the **three-way handshake** (Tomlinson, 1975): each side chooses an initial sequence number and must acknowledge the other\'s, so an old duplicate CONNECTION REQUEST or ACK is recognised and rejected.',
      { img: 'cn-p59' },
      { h: '3. Connection release' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Asymmetric release', items: ['How the telephone works: when one party hangs up, the connection is broken', 'Abrupt: data still in transit may be **lost**'] },
        { title: 'Symmetric release', items: ['Treats the connection as two separate one-way connections, each released separately', 'A side can keep receiving after it has finished sending'] },
      ] },
      { def: 'Two-army problem', text: 'Two blue armies must attack a white army together, but can only send messengers through enemy territory, where they may be captured. No protocol of any number of acknowledgements can make both sides **certain** that the other will attack, because the last message might always be lost. Likewise, no protocol can guarantee that both sides agree to release a connection.' },
      'In practice release uses a **three-way handshake with timers**: host 1 sends DR (disconnection request) and starts a timer; host 2 replies with DR and starts its timer; host 1 sends ACK and releases; host 2 releases when the ACK arrives. If the final ACK is lost, host 2\'s timer releases anyway; if a DR is lost, the sender times out and tries again; after N failures it gives up and releases.',
      { h: '4. Error control and flow control' },
      'As in the data link layer: checksums, sequence numbers, ACKs with retransmission, and sliding windows. The difference is buffering: a host may have many connections, so it **allocates buffers dynamically**. The receiver tells the sender how much buffer space it has (credit), and the sender may send only that much.',
      { h: '5. Multiplexing' },
      { img: 'cn-p61' },
      'Several transport connections can share one network address (**multiplexing**, e.g. many ports on one IP). One transport connection can also use several network paths to get more bandwidth (**inverse multiplexing**, e.g. SCTP).',
      { h: '6. Crash recovery' },
      'If a host crashes, the other side cannot always tell whether the last segment was processed. Tanenbaum shows that no combination of "acknowledge first or write first" strategies is safe in every case: **recovery from a layer N crash can only be done by layer N + 1**, and only if enough state is kept.',
    ],
    terms: [['TSAP', 'Transport service access point: a port.'], ['NSAP', 'Network service access point: an IP address.'], ['Delayed duplicate', 'An old copy of a packet that arrives much later.'], ['Two-army problem', 'Shows that no protocol can guarantee agreement over an unreliable channel.']],
    exam: 'List the elements of transport protocols, then explain each: addressing (TSAP/NSAP with the five-step mail example), connection establishment (delayed duplicate problem and solutions, three-way handshake), connection release (asymmetric vs symmetric, two-army problem, DR/ACK with timers), flow control and multiplexing.',
    revise: ['Addressing, establishment, release, error/flow control, multiplexing, crash recovery', 'TSAP = port, NSAP = IP address', 'Delayed duplicates → limited lifetime + three-way handshake', 'Release: asymmetric loses data; symmetric with DR, DR, ACK and timers'],
  },

  'cn.u4.udp': {
    oneLine: 'UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is the Internet\'s connectionless transport protocol: it adds only ports and an optional checksum to IP in an 8-byte header, with no connection, ordering, flow control or retransmission.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'UDP', text: 'The **User Datagram Protocol** (RFC 768): a **connectionless, unreliable** transport protocol that lets applications send encapsulated IP datagrams without establishing a connection. It provides **process-to-process** delivery with very little overhead.' },
      'UDP transmits **segments** consisting of an 8-byte header followed by the payload. The two ports identify the endpoints within the source and destination machines; when a UDP packet arrives, its payload is handed to the process attached to the destination port.',
      { img: 'cn-udp' },
      { h: 'Header fields (8 bytes)' },
      { table: { head: ['Field', 'Bits', 'Meaning'], rows: [['**Source port**', '16', 'Needed so the receiver can send a reply back to the right process (optional; 0 if unused)'], ['**Destination port**', '16', 'Identifies the receiving process'], ['**UDP length**', '16', 'Length of header + data in bytes; minimum 8, maximum 65,515 (limited by the IP packet size)'], ['**UDP checksum**', '16', 'Optional extra reliability: covers the header, the data and a conceptual IP **pseudoheader** (source and destination IP addresses, protocol number 17, UDP length). If not computed, it is stored as 0.']] } },
      'Including the pseudoheader in the checksum helps detect misdelivered packets, though it violates the protocol hierarchy because IP addresses belong to the IP layer.',
      { h: 'What UDP does not do' },
      { ul: ['No **flow control**, **congestion control** or **retransmission** of bad segments; all of that is up to the user processes.', 'No connection setup and no ordering.', 'What it **does** do: provide an interface to IP with **demultiplexing** of multiple processes by port, and optional end-to-end error detection.'] },
      { h: 'Where UDP is used' },
      { ul: ['**Client-server request/reply** with short messages where setting up a connection is not worth it: **DNS** (port 53) sends one query and gets one reply; if no reply comes, it simply retries.', '**Remote procedure call (RPC)**.', '**Real-time multimedia** with **RTP** (voice, video): a late packet is useless, so retransmission does not help.', '**SNMP** (network management), **DHCP**, **TFTP**, online games, streaming.'] },
      { h: 'UDP vs TCP' },
      { table: { head: ['Point', 'UDP', 'TCP'], rows: [['Connection', 'Connectionless', 'Connection-oriented (three-way handshake)'], ['Reliability', 'Unreliable: no ACKs or retransmission', 'Reliable: ACKs, timeouts, retransmission'], ['Ordering', 'Not guaranteed', 'In order'], ['Flow / congestion control', 'None', 'Sliding window; congestion control'], ['Header', '8 bytes', '20–60 bytes'], ['Data unit', 'Datagram (message boundaries kept)', 'Byte stream'], ['Speed', 'Fast, low overhead', 'Slower, more overhead'], ['Broadcast/multicast', 'Supported', 'Not supported'], ['Applications', 'DNS, RTP, SNMP, DHCP, VoIP', 'HTTP, FTP, SMTP, TELNET']] } },
    ],
    terms: [['Datagram', 'An independent message sent without a connection.'], ['Pseudoheader', 'IP addresses, protocol and length included in the checksum calculation only.'], ['RTP', 'Real-time Transport Protocol, carried over UDP for audio and video.']],
    exam: 'Define UDP, draw the 8-byte header and explain each field (with the pseudoheader for the checksum), list what UDP does not provide, give its applications with reasons, and finish with the UDP vs TCP table.',
    revise: ['8-byte header: src port, dst port, length, checksum', 'Checksum covers pseudoheader + header + data; optional', 'Uses: DNS, RTP, SNMP, DHCP', 'No connection, no order, no flow control'],
  },

  'cn.u4.tcp-intro': {
    oneLine: 'TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) provides a reliable, connection-oriented, full-duplex byte stream between two processes over the unreliable Internet, handling segmentation, retransmission, ordering, flow control and congestion control.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'TCP', text: 'The **Transmission Control Protocol** (RFC 793): designed to provide a **reliable end-to-end byte stream over an unreliable internetwork**. It adapts dynamically to the properties of the internetwork and is robust in the face of many kinds of failures.' },
      'Each machine supporting TCP has a **TCP transport entity** (a library procedure, a user process or, most commonly, part of the kernel) that manages TCP streams and interfaces to the IP layer. It accepts user data streams from local processes, breaks them into pieces of at most 64 KB (in practice often 1460 data bytes, to fit an Ethernet frame) and sends each as a separate IP datagram. At the destination, TCP reconstructs the original byte stream. It times out and retransmits lost datagrams and reassembles out-of-order ones.',
      { h: 'The TCP service model' },
      { ul: [
        'Service is obtained by both sender and receiver creating **sockets**; a socket number is the IP address plus a 16-bit **port**. A connection is identified by the pair of sockets at its two ends: (socket1, socket2).',
        'Ports below 1024 are **well-known ports** reserved for standard services (only privileged users can bind them).',
        'All TCP connections are **full-duplex** (traffic both ways at once) and **point-to-point** (exactly two endpoints). TCP does not support multicasting or broadcasting.',
        'A TCP connection is a **byte stream, not a message stream**: message boundaries are not preserved. Four 512-byte writes may be read as one 2048-byte chunk, two 1024-byte chunks, etc.',
        'When an application passes data to TCP, TCP may send it immediately or **buffer** it to collect more. The **PUSH** flag asks TCP to send without delay; **urgent data** (e.g. Ctrl-C) can be flagged so the receiver is interrupted.',
      ] },
      { img: 'cn-p70' },
      { table: { head: ['Port', 'Protocol', 'Use'], rows: [['20, 21', 'FTP', 'File transfer (data, control)'], ['22', 'SSH', 'Remote login, replacement for TELNET'], ['23', 'TELNET', 'Remote login'], ['25', 'SMTP', 'E-mail'], ['53', 'DNS', 'Name lookup'], ['80', 'HTTP', 'World Wide Web'], ['110', 'POP-3', 'Remote e-mail access'], ['143', 'IMAP', 'Remote e-mail access'], ['443', 'HTTPS', 'Secure Web']] } },
      { h: 'The TCP protocol in outline' },
      { ul: [
        '**Every byte has its own 32-bit sequence number**. Separate 32-bit sequence numbers are carried on packets for the sliding window in one direction and for acknowledgements in the reverse direction.',
        'The sending and receiving entities exchange data in **segments**: a 20-byte header (plus options) followed by zero or more data bytes. Two limits restrict segment size: the 65,515-byte IP payload, and each link\'s **MTU** (path MTU discovery finds the smallest on the route).',
        'TCP uses a **sliding window** protocol with a dynamic window size. When a sender transmits a segment it starts a timer; the receiver sends back a segment (with data if any) whose acknowledgement number is the **next sequence number it expects**. If the timer goes off first, the sender retransmits.',
        'Things that complicate it: segments arriving out of order, segments delayed so long that the sender retransmits needlessly, retransmissions containing different byte ranges than the original. TCP must handle all of these efficiently.',
      ] },
      { h: 'Features of TCP' },
      { table: { head: ['Feature', 'How'], rows: [['Connection-oriented', 'Three-way handshake before data; four-segment release'], ['Reliable', 'Checksums, sequence numbers, cumulative ACKs, retransmission timers'], ['In-order delivery', 'Sequence numbers; out-of-order data is buffered'], ['Full-duplex', 'Data and piggybacked ACKs both ways at once'], ['Flow control', 'Receiver advertises a window (buffer space)'], ['Congestion control', 'Congestion window: slow start, AIMD'], ['Multiplexing', 'Port numbers identify processes']] } },
    ],
    terms: [['Byte stream', 'Data delivered as an ordered stream of bytes without message boundaries.'], ['Well-known port', 'A port below 1024 reserved for a standard service.'], ['Path MTU discovery', 'Finding the largest segment that can cross every link without fragmentation.'], ['PUSH', 'A flag asking TCP to send buffered data immediately.']],
    exam: 'Define TCP and the TCP entity, explain the service model (sockets and ports, full-duplex point-to-point, byte stream, push and urgent), list well-known ports, outline the protocol (byte sequence numbers, segments, sliding window, timers) and tabulate its features.',
    revise: ['Reliable, connection-oriented, full-duplex, point-to-point byte stream', 'Connection = (socket1, socket2); socket = IP + port', 'Every byte numbered; ACK = next byte expected', 'Ports: 21 FTP, 23 TELNET, 25 SMTP, 53 DNS, 80 HTTP'],
  },

  'cn.u4.tcp-header': {
    oneLine: 'Every TCP segment starts with a 20-byte fixed header (up to 60 with options) carrying the ports, 32-bit sequence and acknowledgement numbers, header length, six flags, the window size, a checksum and the urgent pointer.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'TCP segment', text: 'The unit TCP sends: a **fixed 20-byte header**, possibly followed by header **options** (up to 40 bytes), followed by up to 65,535 − 20 − 20 = 65,495 data bytes. Segments with no data are legal and are commonly used for acknowledgements and control messages.' },
      { img: 'cn-tcp-header' },
      { h: 'The fields' },
      { table: { head: ['Field', 'Bits', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**Source port / Destination port**', '16 each', 'Identify the local endpoints. A port plus the host\'s IP address forms a 48-bit unique endpoint; the two endpoints identify the connection (the 5-tuple: protocol, source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port).'],
        ['**Sequence number**', '32', 'The number of the first data byte in this segment (every byte of the stream is numbered).'],
        ['**Acknowledgement number**', '32', 'The **next byte expected** from the other side (cumulative: all bytes before it have been received). Valid only if ACK = 1.'],
        ['**TCP header length**', '4', 'Header length in 32-bit words (5 to 15), because the Options field is variable; it marks where the data begins.'],
        ['(unused / CWR, ECE)', '4', 'Reserved; CWR and ECE are used for explicit congestion notification.'],
        ['**URG**', '1', 'The Urgent pointer is in use.'],
        ['**ACK**', '1', 'The acknowledgement number is valid (set on almost every segment).'],
        ['**PSH**', '1', 'Pushed data: deliver it to the application at once instead of buffering.'],
        ['**RST**', '1', 'Abruptly reset a connection that has become confused (host crash), reject an invalid segment, or refuse a connection.'],
        ['**SYN**', '1', 'Used to establish connections: SYN = 1, ACK = 0 is a CONNECTION REQUEST; SYN = 1, ACK = 1 is CONNECTION ACCEPTED.'],
        ['**FIN**', '1', 'Release a connection: the sender has no more data to send.'],
        ['**Window size**', '16', 'Flow control: how many bytes may be sent, starting at the byte acknowledged. 0 means "stop for now" (a later segment with the same ack and a nonzero window reopens it).'],
        ['**Checksum**', '16', 'Covers the header, the data and a conceptual **pseudoheader** (source and destination IP, protocol 6, segment length), using one\'s complement arithmetic.'],
        ['**Urgent pointer**', '16', 'Byte offset from the sequence number where urgent data ends.'],
        ['**Options**', '0–320', 'Extra facilities: **MSS** (maximum segment size each host will accept, announced at setup; default 536 data bytes), **window scale** (shift the window up to 14 bits for fast long-delay links), **timestamp** (RTT estimation and sequence wraparound protection), **SACK** (selective acknowledgement of ranges received).'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Points examiners look for' },
      { ul: ['The header is 20 bytes **without options**, 60 bytes at most (header length field × 4).', 'Sequence and acknowledgement numbers count **bytes, not segments**.', 'There are **six flags**: URG, ACK, PSH, RST, SYN, FIN.', 'The window field is **16 bits**, so the window is at most 65,535 bytes unless the window scale option is used.', 'Unlike IP\'s header checksum, the TCP checksum covers the **data too**.'] },
      { example: 'A segment with sequence number 1000, 500 data bytes, ACK = 1, acknowledgement number 4001 and window 2000 says: "these are bytes 1000 to 1499 of my stream; I have received everything you sent up to byte 4000; you may send 2000 more bytes starting at 4001".' },
    ],
    terms: [['MSS', 'Maximum segment size: the largest data part a host will accept.'], ['Pseudoheader', 'IP fields included only in the checksum calculation.'], ['SACK', 'Selective acknowledgement: reports byte ranges received out of order.'], ['Window scale', 'Option that multiplies the window size for high bandwidth-delay paths.']],
    exam: 'Draw the TCP header 32 bits wide with every field labelled, then explain each field and each of the six flags. State the minimum and maximum header length and name the main options.',
    revise: ['20 bytes fixed, 60 max', 'Ports 16, seq 32, ack 32, header length 4, flags 6, window 16, checksum 16, urgent 16', 'Flags: URG ACK PSH RST SYN FIN', 'Options: MSS, window scale, timestamp, SACK'],
  },

  'cn.u4.tcp-connect': {
    oneLine: 'TCP establishes a connection with a three-way handshake: the client sends SYN with its initial sequence number x, the server answers SYN + ACK with its own number y and ack x + 1, and the client confirms with ACK y + 1.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Three-way handshake', text: 'The procedure TCP uses to **establish a connection**: three segments that let each side choose its **initial sequence number (ISN)** and confirm that the other has received it. It protects against **delayed duplicate** connection requests.' },
      { img: 'tcp-handshake' },
      { h: 'Steps' },
      { steps: [
        '**Server** passively waits: it executes LISTEN and ACCEPT (passive open), specifying a source or nobody in particular. State: **LISTEN**.',
        '**Client** executes CONNECT (active open), specifying the IP address and port it wants, the maximum segment size, and optionally user data. It sends a segment with **SYN = 1, ACK = 0, seq = x** (its ISN). State: **SYN_SENT**.',
        'The server checks whether a process is listening on the destination port. If not, it replies with **RST** to reject the connection. If so, the process may accept: the server sends **SYN = 1, ACK = 1, seq = y, ack = x + 1**. State: **SYN_RCVD**.',
        'The client acknowledges the server\'s SYN with **ACK = 1, seq = x + 1, ack = y + 1**. Both sides are now **ESTABLISHED** and data can flow both ways. (This third segment may already carry data.)',
      ] },
      'A SYN consumes **one sequence number** even though it carries no data, so the acknowledgement is x + 1, not x.',
      { h: 'Why three segments and not two?' },
      'The network can deliver **old duplicate** segments. Tanenbaum shows the three cases:',
      { img: 'cn-p59' },
      { ul: ['**(a) Normal**: CR (seq = x), ACK (seq = y, ack = x), DATA (seq = x, ack = y).', '**(b) Old duplicate CR appears**: host 2 sends an ACK (seq = y, ack = x); host 1, which is not trying to connect, sends a **REJECT** (ack = y). Host 2 realises it was tricked by a delayed duplicate and abandons the connection.', '**(c) Old duplicate CR and old duplicate ACK**: host 2 replies with seq = y, ack = x; the old ACK arrives with ack = z, not y, so host 2 knows it is an old duplicate and rejects it.'] },
      'Choosing ISNs from a **clock** (rather than starting at 0) means a new incarnation of a connection will not use sequence numbers that old duplicates of a previous one could still carry.',
      { h: 'Simultaneous open' },
      'If two hosts try to connect to each other at the same moment, each sends a SYN and each replies with SYN + ACK; the result is **just one connection**, because connections are identified by their endpoints.',
      { h: 'SYN flood attack' },
      'Because the server must remember the ISN it chose after step 3, a malicious sender can send many SYNs and never complete the handshake, filling the server\'s memory. **SYN cookies** defend against this: the server encodes its state in the sequence number y it sends back (a cryptographic function of the connection), so it remembers nothing until the final ACK arrives.',
    ],
    terms: [['ISN', 'Initial sequence number chosen by each side at connection setup.'], ['Passive open', 'A server waiting with LISTEN/ACCEPT.'], ['Active open', 'A client starting a connection with CONNECT.'], ['SYN cookie', 'A sequence number that encodes server state, defeating SYN floods.']],
    exam: 'Define the three-way handshake, draw it with SYN/ACK flags, sequence and acknowledgement numbers and the states, explain each step, then explain why three steps are needed (old duplicate CR and ACK scenarios). Mention RST, simultaneous open and SYN floods for extra marks.',
    revise: ['SYN (seq x) → SYN+ACK (seq y, ack x+1) → ACK (seq x+1, ack y+1)', 'States: LISTEN, SYN_SENT, SYN_RCVD, ESTABLISHED', 'SYN uses one sequence number', 'Third step rejects old duplicates'],
  },

  'cn.u4.tcp-release': {
    oneLine: 'TCP connections are full-duplex, so each direction is released separately: a side that has no more data sends FIN, the other acknowledges it, and when both directions are shut down the connection is released, with the first closer waiting in TIME_WAIT for twice the maximum packet lifetime.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'TCP connection release', text: 'Releasing a TCP connection by closing each of its two **simplex** directions independently with a **FIN** segment and its **ACK**. TCP uses **symmetric release**: the connection is released only when both directions have been shut down.' },
      { img: 'tcp-release' },
      { h: 'Steps (four segments)' },
      { steps: [
        'The client finishes sending and sends a segment with **FIN = 1, seq = u**. State: **FIN_WAIT_1**.',
        'The server acknowledges it: **ACK, ack = u + 1**. The client → server direction is now closed; the client is in **FIN_WAIT_2** and the server in **CLOSE_WAIT**. The server may still send data: the connection is **half-closed**.',
        'When the server has finished, it sends its own **FIN = 1, seq = w**. State: **LAST_ACK**.',
        'The client acknowledges: **ACK, ack = w + 1**. The server moves to **CLOSED**; the client enters **TIME_WAIT**.',
      ] },
      'The second and third segments are often combined (FIN + ACK), so release frequently takes **three segments**: FIN, FIN + ACK, ACK.',
      { h: 'Why TIME_WAIT?' },
      'The side that closes first waits **2 × MSL** (maximum segment lifetime) before deleting the connection. This ensures that (1) if its last ACK was lost, it is still there to resend it when the other side retransmits its FIN, and (2) all old segments of this connection have died out of the network before the same pair of sockets can be reused.',
      { h: 'Timers protect against lost segments' },
      'To avoid the two-army problem, timers are used: if a response to a FIN does not arrive within **two maximum packet lifetimes**, the sender of the FIN releases the connection anyway. The other side will eventually notice nobody is listening and time out too. This is not perfect, but it is adequate in practice.',
      { h: 'Abrupt release' },
      'A segment with **RST** aborts the connection at once in both directions (for example when an application crashes or a segment arrives for a non-existent connection). Any data in transit is lost: this is **asymmetric release**.',
      { h: 'The TCP connection management states' },
      { table: { head: ['State', 'Meaning'], rows: [['CLOSED', 'No connection is active or pending'], ['LISTEN', 'The server is waiting for an incoming call'], ['SYN RCVD', 'A connection request has arrived; wait for ACK'], ['SYN SENT', 'The application has started to open a connection'], ['ESTABLISHED', 'The normal data transfer state'], ['FIN WAIT 1', 'The application has said it is finished'], ['FIN WAIT 2', 'The other side has agreed to release'], ['TIME WAIT', 'Wait for all packets to die off'], ['CLOSING', 'Both sides have tried to close simultaneously'], ['CLOSE WAIT', 'The other side has initiated a release'], ['LAST ACK', 'Wait for all packets to die off']] } },
    ],
    terms: [['FIN', 'Flag meaning the sender has no more data.'], ['Half-close', 'One direction closed while the other still carries data.'], ['MSL', 'Maximum segment lifetime.'], ['TIME_WAIT', 'State held for 2 × MSL by the side that closed first.']],
    exam: 'Define TCP release, draw the four-segment FIN/ACK exchange with sequence numbers and states, explain the half-closed state, TIME_WAIT and the use of timers, and mention RST for abrupt release. Add the state table for 10 marks.',
    revise: ['FIN → ACK → FIN → ACK (often FIN, FIN+ACK, ACK)', 'Half-close: one side still sends', 'TIME_WAIT = 2 × MSL', 'RST = abrupt release'],
  },

  'cn.u4.tcp-window': {
    oneLine: 'TCP\'s sliding window decouples acknowledgements from buffer space: the receiver advertises how many bytes it can still accept, and techniques like Nagle\'s algorithm and Clark\'s solution stop the window being wasted on tiny segments (silly window syndrome).',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'TCP sliding window (window management)', text: 'Flow control in which every acknowledgement carries a **window size**: the number of bytes, starting at the byte acknowledged, that the receiver is prepared to accept. In TCP, acknowledgements and permission to send more data are **decoupled**.' },
      { img: 'sliding-window' },
      { h: 'Worked example (Tanenbaum)' },
      'The receiver has a 4096-byte buffer.',
      { steps: [
        'Sender sends **2048 bytes** (seq 0). Receiver: ACK = 2048, **WIN = 2048** (half the buffer is used).',
        'Sender sends another **2048 bytes** (seq 2048). Receiver: ACK = 4096, **WIN = 0**: the buffer is full. The sender must **stop**.',
        'The application reads 2048 bytes from the buffer. Receiver sends ACK = 4096, **WIN = 2048**.',
        'Sender may now send up to 2048 more bytes (seq 4096).',
      ] },
      'When the window is 0, the sender may normally not send, with two exceptions: **urgent data** (e.g. to kill a process) may be sent, and the sender may send a **1-byte window probe** to make the receiver re-announce the next expected byte and the window size (to avoid deadlock if a window update was lost).',
      { h: 'Delayed acknowledgements' },
      'Senders are not required to send data as soon as it arrives from the application, and receivers are not required to acknowledge immediately. Delaying ACKs and window updates up to **500 ms** in the hope of getting data to piggyback on reduces load.',
      { h: 'Nagle\'s algorithm (sender side)' },
      'Problem: an interactive editor sends every keystroke as its own segment: 21 bytes of data overhead per byte (a 41-byte packet for 1 byte). Nagle\'s rule: when data comes into the sender one byte at a time, **send the first byte and buffer the rest until the outstanding byte is acknowledged**; then send all the buffered characters in one segment, and repeat. A new segment is also sent if enough data accumulates to fill half the window or a maximum segment.',
      { h: 'Silly window syndrome and Clark\'s solution (receiver side)' },
      'Problem: the sender sends large blocks, but the receiving application reads data **one byte at a time**. The buffer fills; then each time the application reads a byte, the receiver advertises a 1-byte window, the sender sends 1 byte, the buffer is full again, and so on. The connection crawls along one byte per segment: **silly window syndrome**.',
      '**Clark\'s solution**: prevent the receiver from sending a window update for 1 byte. It must wait until it has a decent amount of space available: enough for a **maximum segment** or until its buffer is **half empty**, whichever is smaller. The sender also helps by not sending tiny segments.',
      'Nagle\'s algorithm and Clark\'s solution are **complementary**: Nagle solves the problem of the sending application delivering data a byte at a time; Clark solves the receiving application fetching data a byte at a time. Both aim to send and advertise **large** amounts.',
      { h: 'Cumulative and out-of-order segments' },
      'The receiver may buffer segments that arrive out of order (e.g. it has bytes 0–1023 and 2048–3071) but can acknowledge only the contiguous part (ACK 1024). When 1024–2047 arrives it acknowledges everything up to 3072 at once. The **SACK** option lets it report the out-of-order ranges too.',
    ],
    terms: [['Window probe', 'A 1-byte segment sent when the window is 0, to get a fresh window update.'], ['Nagle\'s algorithm', 'Buffer small writes until the outstanding data is acknowledged.'], ['Silly window syndrome', 'The window and segments shrinking to a few bytes because the receiver reads slowly.'], ['Clark\'s solution', 'Advertise a window only when it reaches an MSS or half the buffer.']],
    exam: 'Define TCP window management, work the 4096-byte buffer example with ACK and WIN values, explain the zero window and probes, then explain Nagle\'s algorithm and silly window syndrome with Clark\'s solution, and say how they complement each other.',
    revise: ['ACK and window size decoupled', 'WIN = 0 → stop; probe with 1 byte', 'Nagle: sender batches small writes', 'Clark: receiver waits for MSS or half buffer before advertising'],
  },

  'cn.u4.tcp-timers': {
    oneLine: 'TCP uses several timers: the retransmission timer (RTO) set from a smoothed round-trip time and its variation, plus the persistence, keepalive and TIME_WAIT timers.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'TCP timer management', text: 'The use of timers by TCP to detect lost segments and keep connections healthy. The most important is the **retransmission timeout (RTO)**: when a segment is sent, a timer starts; if the segment is acknowledged before it expires the timer is stopped, otherwise the segment is **retransmitted** and the timer restarted.' },
      { h: 'Why the RTO is hard to choose' },
      { img: 'cn-rto' },
      'In the data link layer the round-trip time is predictable, so the timeout can be set just above it. In the transport layer the round-trip time to the destination varies widely with congestion. If the timeout is **too short**, needless retransmissions clog the network; if **too long**, performance suffers whenever a packet is lost. The timeout must therefore be **dynamic**, based on continuous measurement.',
      { h: 'Jacobson\'s algorithm' },
      'For each connection TCP keeps **SRTT** (smoothed round-trip time). Each time an acknowledgement comes back, it measures the round-trip time R of that segment and updates:',
      { formula: 'SRTT = α · SRTT + (1 − α) · R          α = 7/8' },
      'Early TCP set RTO = 2 × SRTT, but this did not cope with large variance. Jacobson added a smoothed **variation** RTTVAR:',
      { formula: 'RTTVAR = β · RTTVAR + (1 − β) · |SRTT − R|      β = 3/4' },
      { formula: 'RTO = SRTT + 4 × RTTVAR' },
      'Using 4 times the variation means fewer than 1% of packets arrive later than the timeout. The minimum RTO is 1 second.',
      { example: 'SRTT = 100 ms, RTTVAR = 10 ms, and a new measurement R = 140 ms arrives. RTTVAR = 0.75 × 10 + 0.25 × |100 − 140| = 7.5 + 10 = **17.5 ms**. SRTT = 0.875 × 100 + 0.125 × 140 = **105 ms**. RTO = 105 + 4 × 17.5 = **175 ms**.' },
      { h: 'Karn\'s algorithm' },
      'When a segment times out and is resent, then an ACK arrives, it is unclear whether the ACK refers to the first or the second transmission, so the RTT sample would be wrong. **Karn\'s algorithm**: **do not update the RTT estimate** for retransmitted segments, and **double the timeout** on each successive retransmission until segments get through first time (exponential backoff).',
      { h: 'Other TCP timers' },
      { table: { head: ['Timer', 'Purpose'], rows: [
        ['**Retransmission timer (RTO)**', 'Resend a segment that has not been acknowledged in time.'],
        ['**Persistence timer**', 'Prevents deadlock when the receiver advertises a window of 0 and the later window update is lost. When it expires, the sender transmits a **window probe**; the reply gives the current window. If still 0, the timer is reset.'],
        ['**Keepalive timer**', 'When a connection has been idle for a long time, it fires and makes one side check whether the other is still there. If there is no response, the connection is terminated. (Controversial: it may kill a healthy connection during a transient partition.)'],
        ['**TIME_WAIT timer**', 'Runs for **2 × MSL** after the first side closes, to make sure all packets of the connection have died off before the socket pair is reused.'],
      ] } },
    ],
    terms: [['RTO', 'Retransmission timeout.'], ['SRTT', 'Smoothed round-trip time.'], ['RTTVAR', 'Smoothed round-trip time variation.'], ['Karn\'s algorithm', 'Ignore RTT samples from retransmitted segments and back off the timeout.']],
    exam: 'Explain why choosing the retransmission timeout is hard, give Jacobson\'s formulas for SRTT, RTTVAR and RTO with a numeric example, explain Karn\'s algorithm, and describe the persistence, keepalive and TIME_WAIT timers.',
    revise: ['SRTT = 7/8 SRTT + 1/8 R', 'RTTVAR = 3/4 RTTVAR + 1/4 |SRTT − R|', 'RTO = SRTT + 4 RTTVAR', 'Karn: no RTT sample on retransmits; double RTO', 'Persistence (zero window), keepalive (idle), TIME_WAIT (2 MSL)'],
  },

  'cn.u4.flow-control': {
    oneLine: 'Flow control stops a fast sender from overrunning a slow receiver; TCP does it end to end with a credit-based sliding window, in which the receiver\'s advertised window says how many more bytes it can buffer.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Flow control', text: 'A mechanism that keeps a **fast sender from overwhelming a slow receiver**: the receiver tells the sender how much data it can accept, and the sender never sends more than that. It concerns **one sender and one receiver**.' },
      { img: 'cn-p64' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Flow control', items: ['Problem: the **receiver** is too slow or has too little buffer', 'A point-to-point issue between one sender and one receiver', 'Fix: the receiver advertises a window', 'Picture (a): a fast network feeding a small-capacity receiver'] },
        { title: 'Congestion control', items: ['Problem: the **network** cannot carry all the traffic offered', 'A global issue involving all hosts and routers', 'Fix: senders slow down when the network is overloaded', 'Picture (b): a slow network feeding a large-capacity receiver'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Buffering in the transport layer' },
      'A host may have many connections, each with different traffic, so it cannot reserve a fixed set of buffers per connection the way a data link line can. Options:',
      { ul: ['**Chained fixed-size buffers**: simple, but wasteful when segments vary in size.', '**Chained variable-size buffers**: better memory use, more complex management.', '**One large circular buffer per connection**: good when connections are heavily loaded.'] },
      'If the network is reliable and the receiver always has buffers, the sender need not buffer copies. Otherwise the sender must keep a copy of every unacknowledged segment, and the receiver decides how many buffers to give each connection.',
      { h: 'Credit-based (dynamic) buffer allocation' },
      'Buffer allocation is decoupled from acknowledgement. The sender **requests** buffers based on its needs; the receiver **grants** as many as it can afford (a **credit**). Every acknowledgement carries both the acknowledgement number and the current credit (window).',
      { table: { head: ['Step', 'A → B', 'B → A', 'Comment'], rows: [['1', '< request 8 buffers >', '', 'A wants 8 buffers'], ['2', '', '< ack = 15, buf = 4 >', 'B grants messages 0–3 only'], ['3', '< seq = 0, data = m0 >', '', 'A has 3 buffers left'], ['4', '< seq = 1, data = m1 >', '', 'A has 2 buffers left'], ['5', '< seq = 2, data = m2 >', '', 'Message lost, but A thinks it has 1 left'], ['6', '', '< ack = 1, buf = 3 >', 'B acknowledges 0 and 1, permits 2–4'], ['7', '< seq = 3, data = m3 >', '', 'A has 1 buffer left'], ['8', '< seq = 4, data = m4 >', '', 'A has 0 left and must stop'], ['9', '< seq = 2, data = m2 >', '', 'A times out and retransmits'], ['10', '', '< ack = 4, buf = 0 >', 'Everything acknowledged, but A is still blocked'], ['11', '', '< ack = 4, buf = 1 >', 'A may now send 5']] } },
      'A lost **credit update** could deadlock both sides, so hosts periodically send control segments with the acknowledgement and buffer status (in TCP: the persistence timer and window probes).',
      { h: 'Flow control in TCP' },
      { ul: ['The receiver advertises its free buffer space in the **window size** field of every segment.', 'The sender keeps (LastByteSent − LastByteAcked) ≤ advertised window.', 'A window of 0 stops the sender; a window probe after the persistence timer prevents deadlock.', 'Nagle\'s algorithm (sender) and Clark\'s solution (receiver) avoid silly window syndrome.'] },
      { formula: 'Usable window = advertised window − (bytes sent but not yet acknowledged)' },
      { analogy: 'A tap filling a bucket: the person holding the bucket shouts how much room is left, and the person at the tap never pours more than that.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Credit', 'The number of buffers (or bytes) the receiver grants the sender.'], ['Advertised window', 'The receiver\'s free buffer space announced in each segment.'], ['Deadlock', 'Both sides waiting forever because a window update was lost.']],
    exam: 'Define flow control and contrast it with congestion control (with the two pictures), explain transport-layer buffering and credit-based allocation with the step table, and describe how TCP does flow control with the window field.',
    revise: ['Flow control = protect the receiver; congestion control = protect the network', 'Credit-based: ACK and buffer grant travel together', 'TCP: sent but unacked ≤ advertised window'],
  },

  'cn.u4.congestion': {
    oneLine: 'Congestion is too many packets in the network for its capacity, causing delay and loss; routers fight it with admission control, choke packets, ECN, backpressure, load shedding and RED, and TCP senders adapt with a congestion window using slow start and additive increase, multiplicative decrease.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Congestion', text: 'The situation when **too many packets are present in (a part of) the network**, so performance degrades: delay rises, packets are lost, and goodput falls. When the offered load approaches capacity, queues build up; beyond it, packets are dropped and retransmitted, and in the worst case the network suffers **congestion collapse**.' },
      { def: 'Congestion control', text: 'Making sure the network can carry the offered traffic. It is a **global** issue involving all hosts and routers, unlike flow control, which only concerns one sender and one receiver.' },
      { h: 'Why congestion happens' },
      { ul: ['Packets from several input lines all need the **same output line**: a queue builds up; if there is not enough memory, packets are lost.', 'Adding memory does not help much: with infinite memory, packets wait so long that they time out and **duplicates** are sent, increasing the load further (Nagle, 1987).', '**Slow processors** (routing, queueing, table updates) and **low-bandwidth lines** also cause congestion.'] },
      { h: 'Approaches to congestion control (by time scale)' },
      { flow: ['Network provisioning (slowest)', 'Traffic-aware routing', 'Admission control', 'Traffic throttling', 'Load shedding (fastest)'] },
      { table: { head: ['Approach', 'Idea'], rows: [
        ['**Network provisioning**', 'Build a network that matches the traffic: upgrade links and routers where they are heavily used.'],
        ['**Traffic-aware routing**', 'Make link weights depend on load so traffic shifts away from hotspots (but beware oscillation).'],
        ['**Admission control**', 'In virtual-circuit networks, refuse to set up new connections if the network would become congested.'],
        ['**Traffic throttling**', 'Routers detect approaching congestion (queue lengths, utilisation) and tell senders to slow down.'],
        ['**Load shedding**', 'When nothing else works, routers throw packets away.'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Traffic throttling techniques' },
      { ul: [
        '**Choke packets**: the congested router sends a choke packet back to the source host, telling it to reduce its rate (e.g. by 50%). The original packet is tagged so it does not generate more choke packets on the way.',
        '**Explicit congestion notification (ECN)**: instead of a separate packet, the router **sets a bit in the IP header** of packets it forwards; the receiver echoes it to the sender in its next reply (TCP\'s ECE flag), and the sender slows down.',
        '**Hop-by-hop backpressure**: at high speed over long distances, a choke packet takes too long to reach the source. Instead, the choke takes effect at **every hop** it passes, giving immediate relief at the congested router at the cost of more buffers upstream.',
      ] },
      { h: 'Load shedding and RED' },
      'In load shedding, which packets to drop depends on the application: for file transfer an **old** packet is worth more than a new one (wine policy), while for multimedia a **new** packet is worth more (milk policy). **Random early detection (RED)** drops or marks packets at random **before** the buffer is full, when the average queue length passes a threshold; TCP senders treat the loss as a signal and slow down, avoiding a sudden collapse.',
      { h: 'TCP congestion control' },
      'TCP keeps a second window, the **congestion window (cwnd)**. The sender may transmit at most **min(receiver window, congestion window)** bytes. A **lost packet** (timeout or duplicate ACKs) is taken as a sign of congestion.',
      { img: 'cn-cwnd' },
      { steps: [
        '**Slow start**: the connection starts with cwnd = 1 maximum segment (MSS). Each acknowledged segment adds one MSS, so cwnd **doubles every round-trip time** (1, 2, 4, 8 … segments): exponential growth.',
        '**Threshold**: slow start stops when cwnd reaches the **slow start threshold** (ssthresh), initially large (e.g. 64 KB).',
        '**Congestion avoidance (additive increase)**: above the threshold, cwnd grows by **one MSS per round-trip time**: linear growth.',
        '**Timeout (multiplicative decrease)**: when a segment times out, ssthresh is set to **half the current cwnd**, cwnd is reset to 1 MSS, and slow start begins again.',
      ] },
      { example: 'Tanenbaum\'s example: MSS = 1 KB, threshold 32 KB. cwnd grows 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 KB (slow start), then 33, 34, … 40 KB (one per RTT). A timeout occurs at 40 KB, so the threshold becomes **20 KB** and cwnd drops to **1 KB**. It doubles again up to 20 KB, then grows linearly.' },
      { h: 'Fast retransmit and fast recovery (TCP Reno)' },
      'A timeout is slow to happen. If the sender receives **three duplicate ACKs** (the same acknowledgement number four times), a segment was probably lost while later ones got through: it **retransmits the missing segment at once** (fast retransmit). With **fast recovery**, instead of dropping to 1, it sets ssthresh = cwnd / 2 and continues from there with additive increase, giving the **sawtooth** pattern of **AIMD** (additive increase, multiplicative decrease).',
      { table: { head: ['Event', 'TCP Tahoe', 'TCP Reno'], rows: [['ACK below threshold', 'cwnd doubles per RTT (slow start)', 'Same'], ['ACK above threshold', 'cwnd + 1 MSS per RTT', 'Same'], ['3 duplicate ACKs', 'Fast retransmit; cwnd = 1', 'Fast retransmit; cwnd = ssthresh = cwnd/2 (fast recovery)'], ['Timeout', 'ssthresh = cwnd/2; cwnd = 1', 'Same']] } },
    ],
    terms: [['Congestion window (cwnd)', 'The sender\'s limit based on network conditions.'], ['Slow start', 'Doubling cwnd every RTT from 1 MSS.'], ['ssthresh', 'Slow start threshold where growth turns linear.'], ['AIMD', 'Additive increase, multiplicative decrease.'], ['Choke packet', 'A message from a congested router telling a source to slow down.'], ['RED', 'Random early detection: drop packets early to warn senders.']],
    exam: 'Define congestion and congestion control (and contrast with flow control), give its causes, list the approaches, explain choke packets, ECN, hop-by-hop backpressure, load shedding and RED, then TCP congestion control: slow start, threshold, congestion avoidance, timeout, with the cwnd graph and a numerical example. Mention fast retransmit and fast recovery.',
    revise: ['Send ≤ min(rwnd, cwnd)', 'Slow start: cwnd doubles per RTT to ssthresh', 'Then +1 MSS per RTT', 'Timeout: ssthresh = cwnd/2, cwnd = 1', '3 dup ACKs: fast retransmit (+ fast recovery in Reno)', 'Router side: choke packets, ECN, backpressure, RED'],
  },
}
